## Frontend architecture

This project uses Next.js App Router, React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS, and Vitest.
Use `add-nextjs-feature` when available for feature requests. These rules also
apply when a request only says "add a login screen" or "build a product list".
Use `nextjs-clean-arch` when available for code review or structural refactoring.
A review request is read-only; refactoring preserves behavior, API contracts,
and the established stack unless the user requests those changes.

### Responsibilities and imports

- `src/features/<feature>/domain/`: pure TypeScript business entities, values,
  and invariants. No React, Next.js, network, storage, or environment access.
- `src/features/<feature>/application/`: use cases and the small ports they
  consume. Inject dependencies through function arguments or constructors.
- `src/features/<feature>/infrastructure/`: HTTP/storage implementations,
  external DTO validation/mapping, and platform integrations implementing ports.
- `src/features/<feature>/presentation/`: React components, hooks, view models,
  forms, loading/empty/error states, and Tailwind styling.
- `src/composition/`: construct and connect concrete adapters/use cases. Keep
  server and client composition separate; use request-scoped server dependencies.
- `src/app/`: thin Next.js routes, layouts, Server Actions, and route handlers.
  Compose features and map runtime concerns here, not business policy.
- `src/shared/ui/`: reusable presentation primitives. Shared business values
  belong in a separate framework-free module only when real reuse exists.

Imports point inward: presentation -> application -> domain; infrastructure ->
application ports/domain; composition and app routes assemble the needed layers.
Application code must not import concrete infrastructure or UI. Presentation
receives capabilities/data rather than constructing repositories. Avoid shared
barrels that mix client and server modules or hide an outward dependency.

The `home` feature is a replaceable starter screen. Its empty inner directories
are reserved for real business operations; do not add fake entities or use cases
just to fill them. Remove `.gitkeep` when adding real files.

### Adding features

1. Identify the owning feature and existing API/UI conventions. Preserve route,
   payload, authentication, and styling contracts outside the requested change.
2. Implement business rules and use-case ports only where the behavior needs
   them. Purely presentational changes can stay in the presentation layer.
3. Implement adapters that validate untrusted data, map errors, and propagate
   cancellation. Do not put network requests directly in presentation components.
4. Compose the use cases and connect the route/UI. Add accessible controls and
   loading, empty, error, and success states as relevant to the feature.
5. Add behavioral tests at the affected boundaries and complete the checks below.

### Next.js server/client boundary

Keep pages/layouts as Server Components unless their own behavior needs a client
boundary. Add `"use client"` at interactive leaves. Protect privileged adapter
and server-composition modules with `server-only` when introduced. Never expose
secrets via `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` or pass repositories, callbacks, or class instances
as ordinary Server-to-Client props; use serializable view data. Server Actions
and route handlers validate input and identity, then call use cases. Authorization
must also protect the actual business operation, not only a hidden UI button.

Do not reuse authenticated request state in a process-wide singleton. Make cache
and revalidation decisions explicit for the operation. Preserve the production
Next.js build pipeline; Vite is used for Vitest, not as a replacement app server.

### Tests and verification

- `*.test.ts`: Vitest `unit` project in Node, for domain/use cases/adapters.
- `*.test.tsx`: Vitest `components` project in jsdom, with React Testing Library,
  jest-dom matchers, user-event, and automatic cleanup.
- Mock external ports for use-case tests. Test visible behavior and user actions
  in UI tests, including relevant failure states; avoid snapshots as sole proof.
- Async Server Components and Next runtime semantics require integration/E2E
  coverage when changed. Vitest unit tests alone do not verify that boundary.

```bash
npm run lint
npm run typecheck
npm test
npm run build
```

Lint rejects common framework/outward imports and direct I/O in inner layers.
Also review imports, relative paths, barrels, and Server/Client boundaries;
lint is not a complete architecture proof. Keep the lockfile and use `npm ci`
for repeatable installs. Report skipped runtime checks and existing failures.
